    modelAnswer: "(a) Working Principle: A full-wave rectifier utilizes a center-tapped step-down transformer and two p-n junction diodes D1 and D2. Diodes conduct only in forward bias.\n- During the positive half cycle of AC input, terminal A of the secondary winding is positive and terminal B is negative with respect to the center tap. Diode D1 is forward biased and conducts; diode D2 is reverse biased and does not conduct. Current flows through load resistor RL from top to bottom (X to Y).\n- During the negative half cycle, terminal A becomes negative and terminal B becomes positive with respect to center tap. Now diode D1 is reverse biased (no conduction), while diode D2 is forward biased and conducts. Current flows through load resistor RL in the SAME direction (X to Y).\n\n(b) Waveforms: The input AC wave alternates above and below zero axis. The output across RL is unidirectional pulsating DC containing positive pulses for every half cycle.\n\n(c) Output Ripple Frequency:\nSince both the positive and negative half cycles of the input AC produce consecutive unidirectional output pulses across RL, one full cycle of AC input generates two output pulses.\nTherefore, Output Ripple Frequency = 2 \xB7 Input Frequency = 2 \xB7 50 Hz = 100 Hz.",
    examinerNotes: "Emphasize that current flows through RL in the same direction during both half-cycles. Half-wave output frequency is 50 Hz; full-wave output frequency is 100 Hz."
