    modelAnswer: "Let electrical power P be transmitted over a transmission line of total resistance R at voltage V. The current carried by the transmission line wires is I = P / V.\n\nThe power dissipated as Joule heat in the transmission lines is given by: P_loss = I\xB2 \xB7 R = (P / V)\xB2 \xB7 R = (P\xB2 \xB7 R) / V\xB2.\n\nThis shows that transmission power loss is inversely proportional to the square of the transmission voltage (P_loss proportional to 1 / V\xB2). By stepping up the transmission voltage by a factor of 10 using a step-up transformer, the current is reduced by factor 10, and the transmission power loss in the wires is reduced by a factor of 100 (10\xB2). This minimizes resistive heating losses and prevents large voltage drops across long-distance national grids.",
