    modelAnswer: "Consider a plane wavefront AB incident at angle i on a plane refracting surface XY separating medium 1 (speed of light v1, refractive index n1) and medium 2 (speed v2, refractive index n2, with v2 < v1).\n\nLet time t be the duration taken by point B of the wavefront to reach point C on the interface. Therefore, distance BC = v1 \xB7 t.\n\nDuring this same time interval t, secondary wavelets originating from point A spread into medium 2 with speed v2, traveling a distance AD = v2 \xB7 t. The envelope CD tangent to these secondary wavelets forms the newly refracted plane wavefront.\n\nFrom right-angled triangle ABC: sin(i) = BC / AC = (v1 \xB7 t) / AC.\n\nFrom right-angled triangle ADC: sin(r) = AD / AC = (v2 \xB7 t) / AC.\n\nDividing the two equations:\nsin(i) / sin(r) = [(v1 \xB7 t) / AC] / [(v2 \xB7 t) / AC] = v1 / v2.\n\nBy definition of absolute refractive index: n1 = c / v1 and n2 = c / v2, which implies v1 / v2 = n2 / n1.\n\nTherefore: sin(i) / sin(r) = n2 / n1 => n1 \xB7 sin(i) = n2 \xB7 sin(r).\nThis is Snell's law of refraction.",
    examinerNotes: "Diagram must show right angles at B and D. Time t must be defined as identical for both wavelets."
